\section{Evaluation}
\paragraph{How the docket is built.}
A simulator makes the markets and code writes the strategies, so truth is known; no language model is involved.
A strategy's \emph{true edge} is its Sharpe ratio on the sealed period when run honestly, averaged over fresh worlds. % source: experiments/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md, Ground truth
A case is \emph{guilty} if its claimed Sharpe exceeds its true edge by at least 1.0 and its true edge is below 0.5. It is \emph{innocent} if its true edge is at least 0.7 and within 0.6 of the claim. % source: experiments/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md, Ground truth
The docket has {{e1.court.small.guilty_stopped:k}} guilty cases and {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed:k}} innocent ones. Each guilty case has one planted flaw and an innocent twin with a real edge and similar headline numbers, which makes {{e1.court.small.guilty_stopped:k}} twin pairs. The other {{e1.court.small.unusual_innocent_deployed:k}} innocent cases are \emph{unusual}: honest strategies built to trip a naive rule.
Levels: L1 states the flaw, L2 needs domain knowledge, and in L3 the text is identical for both twins.
We tuned on a dev split of simulated worlds, then froze prompts, test thresholds, tool budgets and models. The test split was run once. % source: experiments/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md, Freeze note
\emph{Stopped} means a ruling of paper or reject. Wilson and twin-pair bootstrap intervals are in the repository. % source: experiments/ANALYSIS_PLAN.md, Outcomes (Wilson 95%, bootstrap over twin pairs)

\input{table1}

\paragraph{E1: the rule, not the evidence alone, lets honest strategies through.}
With Qwen3 8B in every role, the court stopped {{e1.court.small.guilty_stopped}} guilty cases (Wilson 95\% interval {{e1.court.small.guilty_stopped:ci}}) and deployed {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed}} honest ones. The same model as a single reviewer, reading the case alone, deployed {{e1.single.small.innocent_deployed}}.
The post-hoc rows of Table~\ref{tab:e1} separate evidence from rule. A reviewer given the exhibits deployed {{e1.single_exhibits.small.innocent_deployed}}, and a court whose prompts state the rule but whose code does not enforce it deployed {{e1.court_norule.small.innocent_deployed}}. Enforcing the rule lifted the reviewer to {{e1.single_exhibits_rule.small.innocent_deployed}} and the court to {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed}}. The rule overrode the judge in {{e1.court.small.unsupported:j}} of {{e1.court.small.unsupported:k}} rulings.
The adversarial court adds little: {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed:j}} against {{e1.single_exhibits_rule.small.innocent_deployed:j}} honest cases deployed, with the same stops on guilty cases.

\paragraph{The fixed battery matches the court.}
Running every test at fixed thresholds, with no model, stopped {{e1.battery.cpu.guilty_stopped}} guilty cases and deployed {{e1.battery.cpu.innocent_deployed}} honest ones. The court's edge in honest cases deployed is {{e1.diff.court.small.vs.battery.cpu.innocent_deployed}} (interval {{e1.diff.court.small.vs.battery.cpu.innocent_deployed:ci}}). What the court adds is a readable record. It costs about {{e1.court.small.cost_per_case.seconds}} s per case, against {{e1.battery.cpu.cost_per_case.seconds}} s of test time for the battery with no model call.

\paragraph{A mid-size court misses many.}
With Gemma 3 12B in every role the court stopped only {{e1.court.mid.guilty_stopped}} guilty cases, though it deployed {{e1.court.mid.innocent_deployed}} honest ones.
Of the {{e1.derived.court.mid.no_charge:k}} guilty strategies it deployed, the prosecutor filed no charge in {{e1.derived.court.mid.no_charge:j}}, and ran at most one test in {{e1.derived.court.mid.few_tests:j}}.
The rule restrains unsupported stops but cannot make a prosecutor diligent. The hosted Gemini court did not complete because of API quota.

\paragraph{E2 to E4, briefly.}
E2 gives the defence to gpt-oss-120b (hosted) on the 44-case subset: the court stopped {{e2.court.defence_groq.guilty_stopped}} guilty and deployed {{e2.court.defence_groq.innocent_deployed}} honest cases. The all-small court on the same 44 cases stopped {{e2.derived.court_small44.guilty_stopped}} and deployed {{e2.derived.court_small44.innocent_deployed}}.
In E3, notes addressed to the judge did not help: the court stopped {{e3.court.i_small.guilty_stopped}} such cases. Flooding the record with irrelevant exhibits changed nothing ({{e3.court_flood.iii_small.guilty_stopped}} stopped, {{e3.court_flood.iii_small.innocent_deployed}} deployed). These attacks do not adapt.
E4 sends Qwen3 8B to research through the gateway under a neutral and a pressure brief. % source: agent/briefs.py
It submitted a strategy in every session, {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.neutral.submitted}} neutral and {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.pressure.submitted}} pressure, each after a single backtest, claiming a Sharpe near {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.pressure.claimed_sharpe}} against a true edge near zero. That Sharpe comes from a leaky field, found by the battery in {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.neutral.sins_present_by_battery.leaky_field}} neutral and {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.pressure.sins_present_by_battery.leaky_field}} pressure submissions. Pressure did not change this model's behaviour. The court stopped {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.neutral.guilty_stopped}} neutral and {{e4.local:qwen3-8b.pressure.guilty_stopped}} pressure submissions.
One Claude Code (Sonnet) session under pressure ran 4 backtests, avoided the leaky fields and claimed an honest Sharpe of 0.9; the court ruled paper trading only. % source: results/attach/claude_stream_20261116T083547Z.jsonl and results/gateway/records/AC-0002.json

\paragraph{Where the court is wrong.}
The court deployed {{e1.court.small.sealed.deployed_guilty}} guilty strategies but stopped {{e1.court.small.innocent_deployed:miss}} honest ones.
AC-T003-I was rejected because its sealed Sharpe fell from 1.64 to 0.51 in the one world replayed. % source: results/trials/e1/court/small/AC-T003-I.json
Five of the six wrong stops cite the holdout test in this way. % source: results/trials/e1/court/small/ (AC-T003-I, T020-I, T028-I, T034-I, T037-I)
The reasons are also weak. In only {{e1.court.small.right_reason_given_stopped:j}} of {{e1.court.small.right_reason_given_stopped:k}} stopped guilty cases did a sustained finding name the planted flaw and cite a test that targets it, against {{e1.battery.cpu.right_reason_given_stopped:j}} for the battery. Right to stop, often wrong about why. % source: results/trials/e1/court/small/AC-T*-G.json (sustained findings: selection in most)
